#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184993} :activity/id #uuid "c850d730-254f-5cca-b5da-80eca1a9051f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051078 :topic/id #uuid "7f1179a4-dcfa-50b2-b12b-655dfefa3ff3" :topic/math-academy-id 3824} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184993 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185337 :tutorial/content "Let's think about how to complete the square on the following quadratic expression:\n\n$$\n- \\left(x^2 + 6x+4\\right)\n$$\n\nWe begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 + 6x + {\\color{purple}{4}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}}x  + {\\color{purple}{4}}\n$$\n\nThen, we add and then subtract ${\\color{blue}{3}}^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{4}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}}x + {\\color{blue}{3}}^2} - {\\color{blue}{3}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{4}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+{\\color{blue}{3}})^2 - {\\color{blue}{3}}^2+ {\\color{purple}{4}}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+{\\color{blue}{3}})^2 - 5\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}+6x+4) & =-((x+3)^{2}-5) \\\\  & =-(x+3)^{2}+5 \\\\  & =5-(x+3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "50b34082-7844-5fd2-aaaf-8d4a7fe58412" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6162 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "10a00ef6-4086-5bc9-b1f4-a84242b5b363" :step/math-academy-id 37365 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184995}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184995 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184211 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184115 :question/id #uuid "10318f8d-0396-53de-af20-e3b9ee6ac3d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-